  "assessment": {
    "rationale": "Strong on the core unknown-type pressure at the envelope layer: a peer can keep exact bytes, forward opaquely, and return an explicit unsupported-pCID result without speculative parsing. The fit is not perfect because the scenario names an unknown CID codec, while this specimen more directly defines unknown payload/protocol handling inside a known CBOR envelope and leaves unknown signature-protocol behavior less explicit.",
    "strengths": [
      "Explicit unknown-pCID policy allows opaque store-and-forward while forbidding unsafe payload parsing.",
      "Signature coverage of [pcid, payload] binds type identifier to payload bytes.",
      "Layer boundaries are clear: envelope dispatch, payload interpretation, and signature interpretation are separated.",
      "Does not require a central pCID registry."
    ],
    "weaknesses": [
      "Addresses unknown inner pCID more clearly than the literal case of a CID whose outer codec is unknown.",
      "Unknown sig_pCID handling is not spelled out as clearly as unknown payload pCID handling.",
      "Advertisement behavior is implied by opaque forwarding, not explicitly specified."
    ],
    "risks": [
      "Peers may accumulate opaque envelopes they can relay but cannot verify when the signature protocol is also unknown.",
      "Opaque forwarding can propagate harmful or policy-violating content unless local relay/storage policy adds gates.",
      "Draft status means interop details may still shift."
    ],
    "open_questions": [
      "Should unknown sig_pCID follow the same explicit store-forward and no-speculative-parse rule as unknown pCID?",
      "How should peers advertise unsupported objects without implying semantic understanding?",
      "How should this envelope map to the scenario's bare unknown-CID-codec case?"
    ],
    "authority_boundary": "Evidence only; does not settle PromiseGrid design."
  }
